# Advertised rules that the defect-seed suite does not verify, and why.
#
# `packages/pi/src/standin/defect_seeds.zig` pairs a veto-clean baseline with a
# draft that introduces exactly one diagnostic code, and the stand-in gate
# re-derives each outcome through the real veto. A rule that no seed covers is
# a rule nothing in this repository has ever been observed enforcing.
#
# One row per (code, reason slug), with the finding above it. Enforced in both
# directions by scripts/check-rule-coverage.sh: an advertised rule that is
# neither seeded nor listed fails, and so does a row for a code that a seed now
# verifies. The list only ratchets down.
#
# A row is not an exemption and not a plan. It is a claim, checked when it was
# written, that a seed cannot reach this code as the compiler stands today. The
# reason slug says what would have to change first, and every one of them is a
# defect somewhere other than the seed suite:
#
#   no-producer     the registry carries the code but no code path constructs a
#                   diagnostic with it, so nothing can ever emit it
#   shadowed        another checker refuses the construct first, so this rule's
#                   diagnostic never reaches the stream
#   non-default     emitted only under an opt-in mode the veto does not run
#
# Method, so a later reader can repeat it rather than trust it: each row was
# established by probe against the built analyzer, not by reading the registry.
# For `no-producer`, the producing enum variant was traced to its declaration
# and found to have no construction site, then confirmed by writing the handler
# the rule describes and observing the analyzer accept it. For `shadowed` and
# `non-default`, the handler was written, `zts edit-simulate` was run against a
# clean baseline, and the codes it did report are named in the finding.

# POL001, POL003, POL005 and POL007 were in this file with a `no-producer`
# reason and are seeded now. The reason was accurate when written and the fix
# was in the product, not the suite: the allow-list was enforced only at build
# time, through a path that raises `error.PolicyViolation` and prints prose
# carrying no code, so `zts check` and the veto never saw it. `zttp.json` now
# carries a `policy` key and the check path reports coded POL diagnostics.
#
# The four `dynamic_not_allowed` codes are a different case, and their earlier
# `no-producer` reason was wrong. `validateContract` raises them from
# `contract.env.dynamic` and its three siblings, which are set while the
# contract is built - but the contract is never built for such a handler.
# `strict_checker.zig:1532` reports any non-literal argument to a capability
# export as ZTS602 at error severity, and `precompile.zig:1410` returns on
# strict errors before the contract stage. Probe: a computed argument to
# `env`, `cacheGet` and `fetch` each report ZTS602 and nothing else. Four
# evasive shapes - an aliased import, a ternary argument, an argument from a
# call, and an indirect callee held in a record field - are all caught too.
# So the language refuses all dynamic capability access unconditionally, which
# is strictly broader than any allow-list, and the policy knob is vacuous in
# both directions.
POL002 shadowed
POL004 shadowed
POL006 shadowed
POL008 shadowed

# PROP01-PROP06 are the same shape one layer over. `property_diagnostics.zig`
# defines PropertyViolation with no code field at all, so the violations
# precompile collects under -Dverify and writes to violations.jsonl carry no
# code to print. `diagnostic_catalog.propertyCode` is reached only from the
# catalog table.
PROP01 no-producer
PROP02 no-producer
PROP03 no-producer
PROP04 no-producer
PROP05 no-producer
PROP06 no-producer

# ZTS300 and ZTS301 are declared in handler_verifier's DiagnosticKind and
# constructed nowhere. Each appears exactly twice in that file: once in the
# enum, once in the test that asserts the variants are distinct. Probe: an if
# with a non-returning else is accepted, and the shape that does fail reports
# ZTS302, which a seed already covers.
#
# ZTS306 was in this group and is not any more. It was the one row here whose
# rule described a check worth having rather than a duplicate, so it got a
# producer instead of a deletion: `collectImportBindings`, `markUsedImports`
# and `reportUnusedImports` in handler_verifier.zig. It found two genuinely
# dead imports in `packages/tools/tests/fixtures/contract/modules_all.ts` and
# one in this suite's own `clean_literal_access` baseline on its first run.
ZTS300 no-producer
ZTS301 no-producer

# ZTS307 has a construction site, but nothing reaches it through the veto. The
# rule is a verifier warning about a match with no default arm; the strict
# checker and the type checker both refuse that construct first. Probe: a match
# over two number literals with no default reports ZTS205 and ZTS603, and no
# ZTS307. Removing the shadowing would mean changing what the strict profile
# refuses, which is a language decision, not a seed.
ZTS307 shadowed

# ZTS508 is emitted only under the opt-in docs mode. The veto runs the default
# profile, so no seed measured through it can reach the rule. Reaching it needs
# a second analysis mode in the harness, not another draft.
ZTS508 non-default
